1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,489
Your unit tests are green. You deploy. And the buy button does nothing.

2
00:00:05,039 --> 00:00:09,925
The unit tests never opened the page. They tested the code, not what a user sees.

3
00:00:10,475 --> 00:00:13,461
A browser test does what a user does: open the page,

4
00:00:13,461 --> 00:00:14,323
click a button,

5
00:00:14,323 --> 00:00:16,448
and check what appears on the screen.

6
00:00:16,998 --> 00:00:19,695
Today you run browser tests with Play wright.

7
00:00:19,695 --> 00:00:20,834
And when one fails,

8
00:00:20,834 --> 00:00:22,752
you learn to open its recording,

9
00:00:22,752 --> 00:00:24,550
and see exactly what happened.

10
00:00:26,100 --> 00:00:27,529
Here is what to expect.

11
00:00:28,079 --> 00:00:30,981
One: what a browser test is, and when to use one.

12
00:00:31,531 --> 00:00:36,779
Two: finding things on a page, the Play wright way. Locators, and automatic waiting.

13
00:00:37,329 --> 00:00:40,742
Three: evidence. Traces, screenshots, and a report.

14
00:00:41,292 --> 00:00:45,815
Four: the trace viewer, panel by panel. A time machine for a failed test.

15
00:00:46,365 --> 00:00:50,162
Then a live demo, in a real browser, and a lab with six checks.

16
00:00:52,212 --> 00:00:53,577
What a browser test is.

17
00:00:54,127 --> 00:00:56,240
Play wright starts a real browser,

18
00:00:56,240 --> 00:00:57,421
Chrome in our case,

19
00:00:57,421 --> 00:00:59,037
and controls it with code.

20
00:00:59,037 --> 00:01:00,031
It opens a page,

21
00:01:00,031 --> 00:01:00,466
clicks,

22
00:01:00,466 --> 00:01:00,839
types,

23
00:01:00,839 --> 00:01:02,703
and reads what the page shows.

24
00:01:03,253 --> 00:01:05,383
By default the browser is headless.

25
00:01:05,383 --> 00:01:06,783
It runs with no window,

26
00:01:06,783 --> 00:01:07,635
so it is fast,

27
00:01:07,635 --> 00:01:10,677
and it works on a build server that has no screen.

28
00:01:11,227 --> 00:01:13,717
Browser tests are slower than unit tests,

29
00:01:13,717 --> 00:01:15,356
and they break more easily,

30
00:01:15,356 --> 00:01:17,785
because there is more that can go wrong.

31
00:01:17,785 --> 00:01:18,696
So write a few,

32
00:01:18,696 --> 00:01:20,942
for the journeys that matter: search,

33
00:01:20,942 --> 00:01:21,671
add to cart,

34
00:01:21,671 --> 00:01:22,278
check out.

35
00:01:22,828 --> 00:01:26,245
Remember the test pyramid from lesson one thirty-five.

36
00:01:26,245 --> 00:01:28,144
Many unit tests at the bottom,

37
00:01:28,144 --> 00:01:30,359
and a few browser tests at the top.

38
00:01:31,909 --> 00:01:33,381
Finding things on a page.

39
00:01:33,931 --> 00:01:36,490
A locator says which element you mean.

40
00:01:36,490 --> 00:01:40,329
The best locators describe what a user sees: get by role,

41
00:01:40,329 --> 00:01:42,147
with a button and its name,

42
00:01:42,147 --> 00:01:43,157
or get by text,

43
00:01:43,157 --> 00:01:44,235
or get by label.

44
00:01:44,785 --> 00:01:46,349
Avoid long C S S paths.

45
00:01:46,349 --> 00:01:49,612
They break the moment a designer moves a button.

46
00:01:49,612 --> 00:01:52,060
A role and a name survive redesigns.

47
00:01:52,610 --> 00:01:55,063
Then the best feature: automatic waiting.

48
00:01:55,063 --> 00:01:56,201
Before every click,

49
00:01:56,201 --> 00:01:58,774
Play wright waits until the element exists,

50
00:01:58,774 --> 00:01:59,432
is visible,

51
00:01:59,432 --> 00:02:00,210
and is ready.

52
00:02:00,210 --> 00:02:01,527
You never write sleep.

53
00:02:02,077 --> 00:02:03,430
Assertions wait too.

54
00:02:03,430 --> 00:02:03,903
Expect,

55
00:02:03,903 --> 00:02:04,444
locator,

56
00:02:04,444 --> 00:02:05,323
to have text,

57
00:02:05,323 --> 00:02:06,338
keeps retrying,

58
00:02:06,338 --> 00:02:07,893
for up to five seconds,

59
00:02:07,893 --> 00:02:09,448
until the text matches.

60
00:02:09,448 --> 00:02:12,018
These are called web first assertions.

61
00:02:12,568 --> 00:02:16,942
So use the expect from Play wright, not a plain assert, so that the waiting works.

62
00:02:18,492 --> 00:02:22,631
Evidence. When a browser test fails, a one line error is not enough.

63
00:02:23,181 --> 00:02:26,899
A trace is a recording of the whole test: every action,

64
00:02:26,899 --> 00:02:29,535
a picture of the page before and after,

65
00:02:29,535 --> 00:02:30,752
the network calls,

66
00:02:30,752 --> 00:02:31,833
and the console.

67
00:02:31,833 --> 00:02:34,402
It is saved as one trace dot zip file.

68
00:02:34,952 --> 00:02:36,893
Tracing on records every test.

69
00:02:36,893 --> 00:02:40,580
Retain on failure keeps traces only for the failed tests,

70
00:02:40,580 --> 00:02:42,909
which saves space on a build server.

71
00:02:43,459 --> 00:02:48,174
A screenshot, only on failure, gives you a picture of the page at the moment it broke.

72
00:02:48,724 --> 00:02:52,953
And the pie test H T M L report from the last lesson still works.

73
00:02:52,953 --> 00:02:55,295
One command can produce all of them.

74
00:02:55,845 --> 00:02:59,578
Attach the trace to the failed build, and anyone can replay it later.

75
00:03:01,128 --> 00:03:06,120
Go to the lab folder.

76
00:03:07,670 --> 00:03:09,569
Look around.

77
00:03:10,119 --> 00:03:10,884
A shop page,

78
00:03:10,884 --> 00:03:12,032
a folder of tests,

79
00:03:12,032 --> 00:03:14,839
an empty folder called out for the evidence,

80
00:03:14,839 --> 00:03:16,689
and a pie test settings file.

81
00:03:17,239 --> 00:03:21,207
The page and the tests.

82
00:03:21,757 --> 00:03:24,871
The page is just two files: an H T M L page,

83
00:03:24,871 --> 00:03:26,640
and a little java script.

84
00:03:26,640 --> 00:03:29,117
The tests are in test shop dot pie.

85
00:03:29,667 --> 00:03:34,169
Five tests.

86
00:03:34,719 --> 00:03:36,022
Five browser tests.

87
00:03:36,022 --> 00:03:36,708
The title,

88
00:03:36,708 --> 00:03:37,736
adding an item,

89
00:03:37,736 --> 00:03:39,519
the total after two items,

90
00:03:39,519 --> 00:03:40,891
removing everything,

91
00:03:40,891 --> 00:03:43,977
and checkout disabled when the cart is empty.

92
00:03:44,527 --> 00:03:49,157
Run them.

93
00:03:49,707 --> 00:03:52,170
A real Chrome browser ran every test,

94
00:03:52,170 --> 00:03:53,169
with no window.

95
00:03:53,169 --> 00:03:53,968
Four passed.

96
00:03:53,968 --> 00:03:54,701
One failed.

97
00:03:54,701 --> 00:03:56,299
That took a few seconds.

98
00:03:56,849 --> 00:04:01,393
The exit code.

99
00:04:01,943 --> 00:04:05,271
One. At least one test failed, so a pipeline would stop.

100
00:04:05,821 --> 00:04:13,650
Collect evidence.

101
00:04:16,600 --> 00:04:17,551
The same result.

102
00:04:17,551 --> 00:04:20,999
But this time Play wright recorded a trace for every test,

103
00:04:20,999 --> 00:04:22,723
and a picture of the failure.

104
00:04:23,273 --> 00:04:27,219
See the evidence.

105
00:04:27,769 --> 00:04:29,369
One folder for each test.

106
00:04:29,919 --> 00:04:35,445
The failed test.

107
00:04:35,995 --> 00:04:41,093
Two files: the screenshot, and trace dot zip. That zip file is the whole recording.

108
00:04:41,643 --> 00:04:50,667
A report.

109
00:04:51,217 --> 00:04:56,999
This is the same pie test H T M L report from the last lesson, now for browser tests.

110
00:04:57,549 --> 00:05:07,511
Open the trace.

111
00:05:08,061 --> 00:05:12,721
It says it is listening on one twenty-seven dot zero dot zero dot one,

112
00:05:12,721 --> 00:05:14,452
port nine three two three.

113
00:05:14,452 --> 00:05:16,915
The viewer is a small local web page.

114
00:05:17,465 --> 00:05:21,836
The trace viewer opens a failed test as a recording you can walk through,

115
00:05:21,836 --> 00:05:22,615
step by step.

116
00:05:22,615 --> 00:05:24,291
So let us open it in Chrome.

117
00:05:24,841 --> 00:05:25,793
I open Chrome,

118
00:05:25,793 --> 00:05:31,573
and type the address the command printed: one twenty-seven dot zero dot zero dot one,

119
00:05:31,573 --> 00:05:33,409
colon nine three two three.

120
00:05:33,409 --> 00:05:35,721
The viewer loads our failed trace.

121
00:05:36,271 --> 00:05:36,981
Four areas.

122
00:05:36,981 --> 00:05:37,691
At the top,

123
00:05:37,691 --> 00:05:38,530
the timeline,

124
00:05:38,530 --> 00:05:41,628
with small pictures of the page as the test ran.

125
00:05:41,628 --> 00:05:42,402
On the left,

126
00:05:42,402 --> 00:05:45,178
the Actions list: every step the test took.

127
00:05:45,178 --> 00:05:46,081
In the middle,

128
00:05:46,081 --> 00:05:47,114
the page itself,

129
00:05:47,114 --> 00:05:48,792
as it looked at that step.

130
00:05:48,792 --> 00:05:49,953
And at the bottom,

131
00:05:49,953 --> 00:05:51,631
a details panel with tabs.

132
00:05:52,181 --> 00:05:54,678
The Actions list reads like the test,

133
00:05:54,678 --> 00:05:55,555
line by line.

134
00:05:55,555 --> 00:05:56,364
Create page.

135
00:05:56,364 --> 00:05:57,781
Navigate to the shop.

136
00:05:57,781 --> 00:05:59,940
Click on a button named Add mug.

137
00:05:59,940 --> 00:06:02,099
Click on a button named Add cap.

138
00:06:02,099 --> 00:06:02,841
And in red,

139
00:06:02,841 --> 00:06:05,675
the step that failed: expect to have text,

140
00:06:05,675 --> 00:06:06,552
on the total.

141
00:06:06,552 --> 00:06:08,779
Each step shows how long it took.

142
00:06:09,329 --> 00:06:10,229
Click an action,

143
00:06:10,229 --> 00:06:12,762
and the middle shows the page at that moment.

144
00:06:12,762 --> 00:06:14,000
This is the mug click.

145
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:15,464
And this is the cap click,

146
00:06:15,464 --> 00:06:17,265
with the cap button highlighted.

147
00:06:17,815 --> 00:06:18,851
Above the page,

148
00:06:18,851 --> 00:06:19,610
three tabs.

149
00:06:19,610 --> 00:06:22,511
Before is the page just before the action.

150
00:06:22,511 --> 00:06:24,721
After is the page just after it.

151
00:06:24,721 --> 00:06:26,240
Look at the cap click.

152
00:06:26,240 --> 00:06:27,415
Before: one item,

153
00:06:27,415 --> 00:06:28,658
total ten dollars.

154
00:06:28,658 --> 00:06:29,832
After: two items,

155
00:06:29,832 --> 00:06:31,351
and a total of thirty.

156
00:06:31,351 --> 00:06:32,801
Thirty is suspicious.

157
00:06:32,801 --> 00:06:35,564
A mug and a cap should cost twenty-five.

158
00:06:36,114 --> 00:06:37,435
Now the failed step.

159
00:06:37,435 --> 00:06:39,086
The page shows the total,

160
00:06:39,086 --> 00:06:39,548
thirty,

161
00:06:39,548 --> 00:06:40,341
highlighted.

162
00:06:40,341 --> 00:06:42,322
The test expected twenty-five.

163
00:06:42,872 --> 00:06:43,979
The bottom panel.

164
00:06:43,979 --> 00:06:47,106
Call shows the details of the step: the locator,

165
00:06:47,106 --> 00:06:48,539
and the expected text,

166
00:06:48,539 --> 00:06:49,321
twenty-five.

167
00:06:49,321 --> 00:06:51,079
Log is the most useful one.

168
00:06:51,079 --> 00:06:52,969
It shows Play wright waiting,

169
00:06:52,969 --> 00:06:54,271
finding the element,

170
00:06:54,271 --> 00:06:55,183
seeing thirty,

171
00:06:55,183 --> 00:06:56,291
and trying again,

172
00:06:56,291 --> 00:06:56,942
and again,

173
00:06:56,942 --> 00:06:58,701
until five seconds ran out.

174
00:06:58,701 --> 00:07:01,176
That is the automatic waiting at work.

175
00:07:01,726 --> 00:07:03,762
Errors shows the failure message.

176
00:07:03,762 --> 00:07:05,737
Source shows your own test code,

177
00:07:05,737 --> 00:07:08,760
with the failing line highlighted: line nineteen,

178
00:07:08,760 --> 00:07:11,167
the total should have text twenty-five.

179
00:07:11,167 --> 00:07:13,759
Network lists every request the page made,

180
00:07:13,759 --> 00:07:15,857
here only the page and its script.

181
00:07:15,857 --> 00:07:18,942
And Console would show any error from the browser.

182
00:07:19,492 --> 00:07:19,993
Finally,

183
00:07:19,993 --> 00:07:21,685
Metadata shows the browser,

184
00:07:21,685 --> 00:07:22,500
the platform,

185
00:07:22,500 --> 00:07:23,502
the screen size,

186
00:07:23,502 --> 00:07:25,257
and the Play wright version,

187
00:07:25,257 --> 00:07:27,701
so you know exactly where the test ran.

188
00:07:27,701 --> 00:07:32,651
The cause is in the shop's java script: the total is the price times the count,

189
00:07:32,651 --> 00:07:34,406
instead of adding the price.

190
00:07:34,406 --> 00:07:35,909
A browser test found it,

191
00:07:35,909 --> 00:07:37,476
and the trace showed why.

192
00:07:38,026 --> 00:07:39,564
That is the trace viewer.

193
00:07:39,564 --> 00:07:41,593
A time machine for a failed test.

194
00:07:41,593 --> 00:07:42,885
Back to the terminal,

195
00:07:42,885 --> 00:07:43,807
to fix the bug.

196
00:07:44,357 --> 00:07:48,795
Find the bug.

197
00:07:49,345 --> 00:07:53,526
The total is the price times the count. It should add the price to the total.

198
00:07:54,076 --> 00:08:00,348
Fix it.

199
00:08:00,898 --> 00:08:02,775
Now every click adds the price.

200
00:08:03,325 --> 00:08:08,232
Run again.

201
00:08:08,782 --> 00:08:09,827
Five passed.

202
00:08:10,377 --> 00:08:15,007
Exit code.

203
00:08:15,557 --> 00:08:17,583
Zero. The pipeline would go on.

204
00:08:18,133 --> 00:08:20,501
Run the judge.

205
00:08:21,051 --> 00:08:25,041
It checks the evidence, and runs the tests itself. Six out of six.

206
00:08:25,591 --> 00:08:27,553
Clean up.

207
00:08:28,103 --> 00:08:29,959
Removed. Nothing left behind.

208
00:08:31,509 --> 00:08:36,906
Your lab task: run browser tests, collect the evidence, and fix the bug. Six checks.

209
00:08:37,456 --> 00:08:42,832
One: run the tests and save the output. Two: save the exit code of the failing run.

210
00:08:43,382 --> 00:08:48,374
Three: record a trace of every test. Four: take a screenshot when a test fails.

211
00:08:48,924 --> 00:08:52,036
Five: save a self contained H T M L report.

212
00:08:52,036 --> 00:08:55,148
Six: fix the bug in the shop's java script,

213
00:08:55,148 --> 00:08:56,378
not in the tests,

214
00:08:56,378 --> 00:08:58,332
and save the new exit code.

215
00:08:58,882 --> 00:09:01,208
Then lab check, until six out of six.

216
00:09:03,258 --> 00:09:06,053
Where to find the lab. Pause here, and write it down.

217
00:09:06,603 --> 00:09:08,067
One: the repository,

218
00:09:08,067 --> 00:09:12,387
git hub dot com slash it dash waves slash dev ops dash A I,

219
00:09:12,387 --> 00:09:14,731
in the folder academy slash lab.

220
00:09:15,281 --> 00:09:21,105
Two: the files, lab one thirty-eight dot S H, and shortcuts one thirty-eight dot S H.

221
00:09:21,655 --> 00:09:27,116
Three: install it, with sudo install, into slash user slash local slash S bin.

222
00:09:27,666 --> 00:09:29,442
Four: load the short names,

223
00:09:29,442 --> 00:09:32,532
with source shortcuts one thirty-eight dot S H.

224
00:09:32,532 --> 00:09:33,321
Then lab up,

225
00:09:33,321 --> 00:09:33,979
lab check,

226
00:09:33,979 --> 00:09:34,834
and lab down.

227
00:09:35,384 --> 00:09:37,330
Five: you need python three,

228
00:09:37,330 --> 00:09:38,580
and Google Chrome.

229
00:09:38,580 --> 00:09:39,762
Install pie test,

230
00:09:39,762 --> 00:09:42,680
Play wright and pie test H T M L with pip.

231
00:09:43,230 --> 00:09:47,070
Six: only on your own machine. Lab down removes everything.

232
00:09:47,620 --> 00:09:49,263
The link is in the description.

233
00:09:51,313 --> 00:09:54,961
Pass or fail rubric. Six points. All six to pass.

234
00:09:55,511 --> 00:09:58,370
One: the run output says one failed, four passed.

235
00:09:58,920 --> 00:10:01,544
Two: the exit code of the failing run is one.

236
00:10:02,094 --> 00:10:04,248
Three: every test has a trace file.

237
00:10:04,798 --> 00:10:07,124
Four: a screenshot of the failure exists.

238
00:10:07,674 --> 00:10:12,388
Five: the H T M L report is one file, and shows the failing test.

239
00:10:12,938 --> 00:10:17,760
Six: the shop is fixed, the tests are untouched, and the new exit code is zero.

240
00:10:18,310 --> 00:10:19,611
Pass means all six.

241
00:10:21,661 --> 00:10:23,432
Your turn, on your own machine.

242
00:10:23,982 --> 00:10:26,499
One: lab up, and read the instructions file.

243
00:10:27,049 --> 00:10:30,548
Two: run the tests, and save the output and the exit code.

244
00:10:31,098 --> 00:10:34,532
Three: run again with tracing, screenshots, and the report.

245
00:10:35,082 --> 00:10:37,793
Four: open the failing trace with show trace.

246
00:10:37,793 --> 00:10:39,058
Find the failed step,

247
00:10:39,058 --> 00:10:39,540
the log,

248
00:10:39,540 --> 00:10:41,226
and the failing source line.

249
00:10:41,776 --> 00:10:44,976
Five: fix the bug. Then lab check, and lab down.

250
00:10:45,526 --> 00:10:47,361
Pause the video, and begin now.

251
00:11:02,911 --> 00:11:03,786
Quick check.

252
00:11:04,336 --> 00:11:06,746
What is a locator, and which kind is best?

253
00:11:07,296 --> 00:11:08,747
Why do you never write sleep?

254
00:11:09,297 --> 00:11:10,705
What is inside a trace?

255
00:11:11,255 --> 00:11:12,855
Which tab shows the retries?

256
00:11:13,405 --> 00:11:15,674
Objective covered: M C fifteen,

257
00:11:15,674 --> 00:11:16,846
objective three,

258
00:11:16,846 --> 00:11:17,285
bonus.

259
00:11:17,285 --> 00:11:18,310
Browser tests,

260
00:11:18,310 --> 00:11:18,822
traces,

261
00:11:18,822 --> 00:11:20,360
and the trace viewer.

262
00:11:20,910 --> 00:11:22,979
Pause the video, and answer out loud.

